{% comment %}
  Metafield-driven specification table.

  THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES. A technical catalogue lives or dies on specifications:
  purity, particle size, CAS number, dimensions, tolerance. Most stores paste that
  into the description as an HTML blob, which cannot be filtered, compared,
  exported, or marked up for search, because it is prose. Moving it into metafields
  makes each spec a real field, and this renders whatever fields exist without a
  code change per product.

  WHY A DEFINITION LIST AND NOT A TABLE. Semantically these are name/value pairs,
  not tabular data with row and column relationships, and `dl` is what a screen
  reader announces correctly. It also collapses to one column on a phone with no
  horizontal scroll, which a real `table` will not do without a wrapper.

  FOUR BUGS FIXED AFTER REVIEW, each of which looked fine until it did not:

    1. NO SECOND Product NODE. This used to emit its own
       {"@type":"Product","@id":"...#product","additionalProperty":[...]}, on the
       belief that Google merges nodes sharing an @id. The theme's real Product
       node carries no @id at all, so nothing matched and the page shipped TWO
       Product entities, the second with no `name`, which is the one hard
       requirement Product has. The specs now render as a visible list only, and
       the structured-data extension belongs in the theme's own Product node where
       it can be a single entity.

    2. BOOLEAN false IS NOT BLANK, EXCEPT IT IS. Liquid compares `false` equal to
       `blank`, so a `{% if field.value != blank %}` guard silently dropped every
       boolean spec whose value was false, and the "No" branch below was
       unreachable. A "Hazardous: No" row simply vanished, which on a technical
       catalogue is the row that most needs to be there.

    3. TYPED VALUES GO THROUGH ONE RENDERER. Iterating a namespace yields
       [key, Metafield drop] pairs, and the drop prints as an object for a
       dimension, a rating, a reference or rich text. That is handled once in
       snippets/spec-value.liquid rather than twice, differently.

    4. LABELS ARE NOT `capitalize`d BLINDLY. `cas_number` became "Cas number" and
       `ph_range` became "Ph range", mangling exactly the vocabulary this section
       exists to display. A label override setting handles the acronyms, and
       capitalize is only the fallback.

  Blank-safe by construction: the whole section renders nothing when the namespace
  is empty, so it can sit in the default product template for every product without
  leaving an orphan heading on the ones that have no specs.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign ns = product.metafields[section.settings.namespace]
-%}

{%- if ns != blank -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Styles BEFORE the markup. Emitting them after means the grid exists unstyled
    for a beat, which is a layout shift on a product page for no reason.
    Scoped to this section's id, so two instances cannot fight and the rules do
    not leak into the rest of the theme.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- style -%}
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table {
      padding-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px;
      padding-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;
    }
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__inner {
      max-width: var(--page-width, 1200px);
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 0 1.5rem;
    }
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__heading {
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
    }
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__list {
      margin: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.25);
      /* Capped to a readable measure. Measured on the references: Fisher runs a
         296px label inside a 651px container and Edmund is similar, so the pair
         stays visually joined. Left uncapped this list spans the full 1280px page
         and the value lands 350px from its own label, which reads as two
         unrelated columns. */
      max-width: 44rem;
    }
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__group-heading {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
      opacity: 0.7;
      margin: 1.4rem 0 0.4rem;
    }
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__group:first-of-type
      .spec-table__group-heading { margin-top: 0; }
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__row {
      display: grid;
      /* The pair stays SIDE BY SIDE at every width, label column at 38%.
         Measured on the references: McMaster holds 38/62 at 374px and Edmund
         about 50/50 at 390px. Stacking on a phone, which this used to do under
         480px, turns a 20-spec table into a 40-row scroll on the device where
         scanning is hardest. */
      grid-template-columns: 38% 1fr;
      gap: 0.75rem;
      padding: 0.6rem 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.25);
    }
    @media screen and (max-width: 480px) {
      #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__inner { padding: 0 1rem; }
      #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__row {
        gap: 0.5rem;
        font-size: 0.95rem;
      }
    }
    /* The VALUE carries the contrast, not the label. Both reference sites
       disagree on how to treat the label and agree on this: the number someone
       came for is the darkest text in the row, and the label is furniture.
       Reversing it, which is what this section did first, buries the data. */
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__key {
      margin: 0;
      font-weight: 500;
      opacity: 0.72;
    }
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__value {
      margin: 0;
      /* Full-contrast, through the THEME'S OWN variable rather than a hex, so a
         merchant changing their colour scheme still gets a readable value.
         Measured: the theme paints body text at rgba(0,0,0,0.75) and exposes
         --color-foreground as 0, 0, 0, so the value goes to the full foreground
         while the label sits below the body weight. That is the hierarchy both
         reference sites use. */
      color: rgb(var(--color-foreground, 0 0 0));
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* A value that is a real link keeps looking like one. */
    #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .spec-table__value a { color: revert; }
  {%- endstyle -%}

  <div class="spec-table">
    <div class="spec-table__inner">
      {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
        <h2 class="spec-table__heading">{{ section.settings.heading | escape }}</h2>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        Grouping pass. A key written `physical__weight_grams` puts the row under a
        "Physical" heading; a key with no prefix falls into the default group, so
        an existing catalogue needs no migration to keep working.

        Each group is its own dl under its own heading rather than a full-width
        band row inside one table, which is how Edmund does it visually but
        without the semantics: a banded td colspan groups nothing for a screen
        reader.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign group_names = ''
        for pair in ns
          assign parts = pair[0] | split: '__'
          if parts.size > 1
            assign gname = parts[0] | replace: '_', ' ' | capitalize
          else
            assign gname = section.settings.default_group | default: 'General'
          endif
          unless group_names contains gname
            if group_names == ''
              assign group_names = gname
            else
              assign group_names = group_names | append: '~|~' | append: gname
            endif
          endunless
        endfor
        assign groups = group_names | split: '~|~'
      -%}

      {%- for group in groups -%}
      <div class="spec-table__group">
        {%- if groups.size > 1 -%}
          <h3 class="spec-table__group-heading">{{ group | escape }}</h3>
        {%- endif -%}
      <dl class="spec-table__list">
        {%- for pair in ns -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign gparts = pair[0] | split: '__'
            if gparts.size > 1
              assign row_group = gparts[0] | replace: '_', ' ' | capitalize
            else
              assign row_group = section.settings.default_group | default: 'General'
            endif
          -%}
          {%- if row_group != group -%}{% continue %}{%- endif -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign key_parts = pair[0] | split: '__'
            if key_parts.size > 1
              assign key = key_parts | last
            else
              assign key = pair[0]
            endif
            assign field = pair[1]

            # `false` compares equal to blank in Liquid, so a boolean has to be
            # allowed through explicitly or every "No" disappears.
            assign renderable = false
            if field.type == 'boolean'
              assign renderable = true
            elsif field.value != blank
              assign renderable = true
            endif
          -%}
          {%- if renderable -%}
            {%- liquid
              # An override lets a merchant write "CAS number" or "pH range" for a
              # key that capitalize would otherwise mangle. Format is
              # key:Label, one pair per line.
              assign label = key | replace: '_', ' ' | capitalize
              assign overrides = section.settings.label_overrides | newline_to_br | split: '<br />'
              for line in overrides
                assign bits = line | strip | split: ':'
                if bits.size == 2
                  assign override_key = bits[0] | strip
                  if override_key == key
                    assign label = bits[1] | strip
                  endif
                endif
              endfor
            -%}
            <div class="spec-table__row">
              <dt class="spec-table__key">{{ label | escape }}</dt>
              <dd class="spec-table__value">
                {%- render 'spec-value', field: field -%}
              </dd>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </dl>
      </div>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Specification table",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "section-spec-table",
  "enabled_on": {
    "templates": ["product"]
  },
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "Specifications"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "namespace",
      "label": "Metafield namespace",
      "default": "specs",
      "info": "Every metafield in this namespace is rendered, in the order Shopify returns them. Adding a spec needs no theme change."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "default_group",
      "label": "Default group heading",
      "default": "General",
      "info": "Used for metafields with no group prefix. A key written physical__weight groups under Physical."
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "label_overrides",
      "label": "Label overrides",
      "info": "One per line as key:Label, for example cas_number:CAS number. Without an override the key is title-cased, which mangles acronyms.",
      "default": "cas_number:CAS number\nph_range:pH range"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_top",
      "label": "Top padding",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 32
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_bottom",
      "label": "Bottom padding",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 32
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Specification table"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
